[h1]ZeD-GUI [B42][/h1]
Built for my fellow humans and zombies alike. For the love of the game.

Doidbros and Buzzlord present ZeroDay Graphical User Interface, ZeD-GUI for short. It's a rebuild of Project Zomboid's whole interface: inventory, crafting, building, health, maps, the HUD and the menus.

It doesn't change how the game plays. Same rules, same balance, same systems. That makes it safe for a hardcore run or a builder save alike. It only changes how you get at things: easier to use, easier to read.

[h2]How it's laid out[/h2]
Most things live in two windows.

[b]The character window[/b] is you: your inventory, what you're wearing, health, temperature, who you are, your journal, skills, the radio you carry.

[b]The world window[/b] is everything around you: containers, crafting at whatever bench or station is near, building, maps, foraging, animals, the car you're standing next to.

Whatever you're dealing with opens in the window you'd expect. Either window can be collapsed, rolled up, hidden or dragged out of the way.

[h2]How you use it[/h2]
Controller and keyboard and mouse are equals. Every action has at least two ways to do it, and the aim is for both to be predictable: things should work the way you'd expect. When something doesn't, that's a bug, and I want to hear about it.

[h2]Make it yours[/h2]
Grid or list inventory, color themes, text size, interface scale, where things sit on screen. There's a lot. Poke around until it suits you. Most of the settings are under Options, Mods, ZeD-GUI.

[h2]Why it looks like this[/h2]
The game is set in 1993, so the interface is dressed like the computers of the time, to keep you in that world. The fonts are humanist faces picked because their letters are easy to tell apart, so it reads easier for more people.

[h2]Before you install[/h2]
[list]
[*]Build 42 only. Tested in single player. Multiplayer hasn't been tested yet; if you try it on a server, tell me how it went.
[*]It hides vanilla's windows. Mods that add buttons to vanilla's inventory or character window lose those buttons. Mods that work through the game's own systems (right-click menus, container buttons, items, recipes) are fine.
[*]It adds one item, a steel digital watch. Take ZeD-GUI off mid-save and those watches go with it. Everything else is interface and safe to add or remove.
[*]English only for now. Translators are welcome; see the Translations thread.
[/list]

[h2]When it breaks[/h2]
Post in the Bug reports thread rather than the comments, which scroll away. Include your game version, ZeD-GUI's version (on the desk: ZeD menu, About), your mod list, what you did, and your console.txt (C:\Users\you\Zomboid\console.txt) on a paste site.

Ideas and requests go in the Requests thread. Making an add-on: the Add-on makers thread.

[h2]Servers[/h2]
Workshop ID: (after upload)
Mod ID: ZeD_UI
[code]WorkshopItems=(Workshop ID)
Mods=ZeD_UI\[/code]
Every player needs it, since it adds an item.

[h2]Who made this, and how[/h2]
No one reads this far into a description, so here's the honest part.

I've been trying to fix this game's interface since Build 41 went stable. I didn't know what any of the code did. I'd change some values to get different colors, make some graphics, watch it fail, and try again. Two attempts at this for Build 41 didn't make it.

I tried recruiting people to help, and talked to other modders who'd taken on projects like this. In the end it came down to doing it myself, or it was never getting done. This is the third attempt, and the first time the tools I needed to finish it existed.

This version alone took well over 300 logged hours. That doesn't count the mistakes, the frustration, or the time spent thinking through problems away from the keyboard.

Those tools are AI. The ideas, the systems and the design are mine. Claude wrote the code to my plans, ran the QA testing and the security and accessibility checks, and did the research, including a lot of "can the game's Java and Lua even do this?" Nothing Claude made went in untouched. Every piece got picked apart, reworked and bent until it matched what I had in my head. If AI used as a tool by one person, trying to build something this size in a year, doesn't sit right with you, you're completely free not to use it.

Hermit the Trog designed this. An inanimate animated object. Mad scientist, daydreamer, professional do-nothinger, self-designated boofhead, and purveyor of remedies for hobsnobberies, hogwashers and gobblygunks. Big believer in AI as a tool, bigger believer in the human touch. AI is the boat. I'm the captain.

It'll be supported, or up for adoption should something happen to the Hermit. Find me on GitHub: [url=https://github.com/hermit-the-trog]github.com/hermit-the-trog[/url]. Hermit is good at sharing but doesn't work well with others. Now get off my lawn.
